Subject: Size an array field from the type its elements are written as

`<div rustbindgen replaces="X"></div>` binds `X` as some other type the header
declares, and that other type is usually a different size: the annotation
exists to substitute a Rust-shaped stand-in for a C++ type Rust cannot express.
bindgen keeps the layout of a struct with such a field honest by asking
`StructLayoutTracker` about the type the field is *written* as - resolved
through the `ResolvedTypeRef` the replacement is recorded as - and padding the
struct out to the size clang reported for it.

An array of that field is not covered.

    struct Real { unsigned long a; unsigned long b; unsigned long c; };
    /** <div rustbindgen replaces="Real"></div> */
    struct Stand { void* p; };
    struct A { Real a[3]; };
    struct B { Real a; };

`B` pads: its one field is written as `Stand`, eight bytes, and sixteen bytes
of padding follow it. `A`'s field is written as `[Stand; 3]`, twenty-four bytes,
but the layout handed to the tracker is the one clang measured for the array -
three `Real`s, seventy-two bytes. The tracker concludes the field filled the
struct and emits no padding at all, so `A` is a third of the width of the
object C++ hands it. Every `A` crossing the boundary is truncated, and a caller
who asks C++ to construct one in storage Rust sized gets it built past the end.

So size an array field the way every other field is already sized: from the
type its elements are written as. The element goes through the same walk the
scalar case uses, which is what makes the substitution visible, and the walk
repeats down each array so that an array of arrays composes. The size is
`N * align_to(element.size, element.align)` - the formula the
over-aligned-element correction below already used, which now reads the one
layout both want.

Where the element is written as the type it was declared with, this is the
layout clang reported: `sizeof(T[N])` is `N * sizeof(T)`, `sizeof(T)` is
already a multiple of `alignof(T)`, and `alignof(T[N])` is `alignof(T)`. For
those arrays the computed size and alignment are unchanged. The exception is an element the
walk resolves *through* to something of a different alignment - a typedef
carrying a reduced `aligned` attribute is one - which is the walk's existing
behaviour rather than this arm's, and which the emitted alias already did not
carry.

The over-aligned-element correction keeps its own job, clamping an alignment
bindgen cannot promise, and now reads that alignment out of the field's layout
rather than resolving the element a second time. The two agree for an array
written as one. They part where the walk stopped short of the array - at an
opaque alias over one - and there the field is written as a blob of the alias's
own layout, which is the layout the clamp is then handed.

--- a/codegen/mod.rs
+++ b/codegen/mod.rs
@@ -1736,6 +1736,56 @@
         item = next;
     }
     item
+}
+
+/// The layout of the Rust type written for a field of type `ty`.
+///
+/// For anything but an array that is the layout of what `field_representation`
+/// named, because what codegen writes for the field is that item's type. An
+/// array is the exception. `[T; N]` is written by converting the element and
+/// repeating it, so its size is `N` times the size of the type the *element* is
+/// written as - and a substituted element does not share a size with the
+/// element C++ declared, which is the size clang measured for the array. So ask
+/// for the element's representation in turn, which also makes an array of
+/// arrays compose.
+fn field_representation_layout(
+    ctx: &BindgenContext,
+    ty: &Type,
+) -> Option<Layout> {
+    array_representation_layout(ctx, ty, &mut HashSet::default())
+}
+
+/// The recursion behind `field_representation_layout`, carrying the elements it
+/// has already measured.
+///
+/// Each stop leaves the array sized as clang measured it, which is what it was
+/// sized as before any of this. There are three. An element with no layout has
+/// no size to multiply. A product no `usize` holds is not a size either, and a
+/// replacement wider than what it replaces can reach one from a declaration
+/// clang accepted. And an element already on the way down is a cycle: C++ has
+/// no array whose element is itself, but a `replaces=` annotation naming an
+/// array of the type it replaces builds one out of two declarations that are
+/// each fine on their own, and a walk with no memory would not come back.
+fn array_representation_layout(
+    ctx: &BindgenContext,
+    ty: &Type,
+    measured: &mut HashSet<ItemId>,
+) -> Option<Layout> {
+    if let TypeKind::Array(element, len) = *ty.kind() {
+        let element = field_representation(ctx, element);
+        if measured.insert(element.id()) {
+            if let Some(layout) = element.as_type().and_then(|element| {
+                array_representation_layout(ctx, element, measured)
+            }) {
+                let stride =
+                    struct_layout::align_to(layout.size, layout.align);
+                if let Some(size) = stride.checked_mul(len) {
+                    return Some(Layout::new(size, layout.align));
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    ty.layout(ctx)
 }
 
